        "original_id": "5ac3390e-7b80-549a-8239-8cd4734b4da8",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Events: Swiss immigration referendum, February 2014\n- Concepts: Swiss federal popular initiative against mass immigration, Switzerland–European Union relations, bilateral treaties, immigration quotas, single market\n\nFacts:\n- In 2002, bilateral treaties between Switzerland and the European Union were launched.\n- The Swiss Confederation adopted various provisions of European Union law through a series of bilateral treaties in order to participate in the European Union's single market.\n- The Swiss federal popular initiative \"against mass immigration\" was a referendum that aimed to limit immigration through quotas.\n- The Swiss federal popular initiative \"against mass immigration\" sought to restore a quota-based immigration system like the one used before the 2002 bilateral treaties between Switzerland and the European Union.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "76b25a22-c1d1-50d5-9a02-9e310baa8ada",
        "text": "Page: Swiss immigration referendum, February 2014. Sentence: The Swiss federal popular initiative \"against mass immigration\" (German: \"Eidgenössische Volksinitiative \"Gegen Masseneinwanderung\"\" , French: \"Initiative populaire « Contre l'immigration de masse »\" , Italian: \"Iniziativa popolare \"Contro l'immigrazione di massa\"\" ) was a referendum that aimed to limit immigration through quotas, as it had been prior to the bilateral treaties between Switzerland and the European Union launched 2002.\nPage: Switzerland–European Union relations. Sentence: The relations between Switzerland and the European Union (EU) are framed by a series of bilateral treaties whereby the Swiss Confederation has adopted various provisions of European Union law in order to participate in the Union's single market.",
